um when it's an employee market i think  it sends um it sends a message to us  that  we took a lot of things for granted you  know people need jobs we can pay them  fairly we don't have to pay them better  than sort of middle you know um the the  the kinds of things that we can offer  them become uh you know secondary  because you know we have all the cards  we have all the power um and so i think  one of the things that forces us to do  is to start looking at what people get  from work the nature of work has changed  over the course of decades you know work  wasn't the place that we had our sense  of belonging people you know if you go  back to the 50s or 60s work was a place  you made a living  and you had your sense of belonging and  community from literally your community  you would whether it was much more  people were much more active at church  um we went to bowling leagues um we were  members of shriners clubs and volunteer  groups like we had strong senses of  community and and  and belonging outside of work and over  the course of the years those things  have sort of blended you know  we've seen less engagement in church  bowling leagues have  all but disappeared um and now people  are demanding things that they never  used to demand from work things like  belonging things like what am i being a  part of like that my personal purpose  has to be intertwined with with the  organization  and so i think companies can start to  really look at themselves and say do we  actually have a purpose that goes beyond  our financial uh results do we actually  have a purpose that people can feel a  part of do we have metrics that help  people see that we're making progress in  things other than what we're offering  a shareholder or what the bottom line is  producing are our incentive structures  um promoting things like teamwork is our  training going to give you skills like  how to communicate and how to listen  that will benefit you of course at work  but you'll have a better relationship  with your spouse and your kids because  of what you're learning from your work  and when we're sitting down and talking  to people and interviewing and  advertising our jobs are we saying come  work for this great job lots of  opportunity upward mobility get a great  salary or are we saying  we um we want to make you a better  version of yourself  and start looking at some of alternative  things that people used to get outside  of work that they want from work now  that are we actually building that into  our work life culture  and i say work life culture as if they  are one thing so i think i think we can  actually  it starts quite frankly by listening  which is call the people who have left  and say can you tell me not why you left  can you tell me what what you're leaving  for  um you know what are you pursuing and  then everything they say don't have an  answer don't say well we already do that  don't try and fix something just say go  on tell me more i don't understand go on  tell me more and really get a full  picture of what's going on in people's  lives and what their ambitions and  dreams are because i would i would bet  money that um  that a lot of the things that people  feel like they're lacking that they're  looking for that they're searching for  probably at least some of it we could  help them find at work but the problem  is we don't promote those things even in  our recruitment ads you know  why don't we start talking about our  vision our mission and how we help  people grow before we talk about upward  mobility salary and where you want to  work  so i think we can start looking at some  asymmetrical things that we didn't use  to look at quite frankly they've always  been there we just didn't need to look  at them
 

